-- title:
--   Structure theorem for finitely generated modules over a PID
-- statement:
--   **The structure theorem for finitely generated modules over a principal ideal domain.**
--
--   Every finitely generated module $M$ over a PID $R$ decomposes as
--   $$M \;\cong\; R^n \;\oplus\; \bigoplus_{i} R/(p_i^{e_i})$$
--   with $n$ the free rank and each $p_i$ irreducible.
--
--   A free part plus a torsion part, and the torsion part splits into primary cyclic pieces. This
--   single statement specialises to two of the most-used classification theorems in algebra:
--   over $R = \mathbb{Z}$ it is the classification of finitely generated abelian groups, and over
--   $R = k[X]$ applied to a vector space made into a $k[X]$-module by a linear operator it yields
--   rational and Jordan canonical forms, with the $p_i^{e_i}$ becoming elementary divisors.
--
--   Principality is what makes it work — the same statement fails over $\mathbb{Z}[X]$ or
--   $k[X,Y]$, where finitely generated modules can be far more complicated. The invariants
--   ($n$ and the multiset of $p_i^{e_i}$) are uniquely determined, though that uniqueness is a
--   separate statement from the existence asserted here.
--
--   **Formalization note.** `Fin n →₀ R` is the free module of rank `n`, `⨁` is the direct sum,
--   and `R ∙ p i ^ e i` is the ideal generated by $p_i^{e_i}$. The result is Mathlib's
--   `Module.equiv_free_prod_directSum`.
-- source:
--   Listed in Mathlib's "1000 theorems" manifest (docs/1000.yaml); formalized in Mathlib. Proof here reduces to the corresponding Mathlib result.

import Mathlib

namespace FamousTheorems

universe u v

open Filter Set Topology DirectSum

theorem structure_theorem_pid {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsPrincipalIdealRing R] [IsDomain R]
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] [Module.Finite R M] :
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (ι : Type u) (_ : Fintype ι) (p : ι → R) (_ : ∀ i, Irreducible <| p i) (e : ι → ℕ),
      Nonempty <| M ≃ₗ[R] (Fin n →₀ R) × ⨁ i : ι, R ⧸ R ∙ p i ^ e i := by sorry

end FamousTheorems
